still quinces with us do you remember  opening your exam results i do 22 or so  years ago i remember the nervousness  sort of heading into school to get them  yeah when i did it it used to be  actually pinned up on the wall so  everybody could see what your results  were  yeah i know  thankfully i did well in english not so  good in maths uh what is the overall  picture looking like so we'll have to  wait and see um so the results will be  out very very soon but i think the first  thing is date is today is a celebration  of all those young people's achievements  and efforts over the course of the past  two years so today is about saying huge  well done and a huge thank you to all of  the teachers and the school leaders and  of course parents and carers that are  supported young people to get those  results and you know i wish them every  success with what they go on to do next  the grades are going to be lower though  aren't they so yet they are and and  that's very much part of part of the  plan so over the last couple of years  we've had teacher assess grades we've  gone back for the first time to  examinations but we've done that in a  staged way so it's not just back to pure  examinations we recognize the fact that  young people have faced huge disruption  of the past couple of years so there  have been adaptations in place and  ofqual have reflected it in their  marking and grading  but looking up um what we put into  catch-up funding as opposed to what  other countries do uh the netherlands um  catch up funding per pupil was 2100  pounds ours was 310 pounds how can that  be satisfactory so i think it's  difficult to make international  comparisons in that way because we have  very different systems we put in place  around 5 billion pounds of support  and that's targeted to those who needed  it the most um  we we keep a real watchful eye on these  things and we continue to look at exam  results and things like our education  improvement areas around the country  looking at how we can  reduce the  attainment  attainment gap and make sure wherever  you live in this country you're getting  a world-class education and the same  opportunity to thrive okay let's keep it  within our borders then if you don't  want to compare it to other countries  close to us  the then  tsar sir kevin collins who had been  asked to come up with his view on how  much government spending should be to  try to improve our education system he  said 15 billion pounds worth of funding  that was totally rejected and he  resigned as a result he did we put in  place uh around five billion pounds of  funding i'm not gonna say i said but  that's 10 billion less  compared to what he what he asked for at  that time and that's of course the start  we keep this under review and the  previous education secretary and the  current education secretary has said the  same we keep a watchful eye on these  things we continue to invest in  education and if we need to invest more  of course we will but i think what  today's about is about celebrating those  gcse results and these young people up  and down the country that worked  incredibly hard we've made adaptations  deliberately to reflect the fact that  young people have faced that disruption  but if you look at the national tutoring  program so some 1.8 billion pound  program which continues now uh that  delivered around 2 million courses up  and down the country those who we know  have got the shortest amount of time  left in education so the 16 to 19 year  olds they're getting those extra 40  hours at a cost of some 800 million so  the investment is going in we keep our  watch for life we need to invest more i  have no reservations about going to the  treasury and asking for that so it's  wrong to say that we invest less in  students now than we did in 2010.  so i think that  there are competing arguments on this in  terms of  whether you you sort of budgeted  inflation for that but of course you  know we continue to invest i think  around an additional 7 billion pounds  over this sr period so an extra 4  billion pounds into our school budget  this year i think the overall education  budget is around 86  billion pounds so we do invest huge  amounts into into education but as i say  today is about celebrating the success  of all of those gcse students up and  down the country and who are looking  forward to going on taking their next  steps in education what are you putting  regional disparities down to  so i think there have always been uh  regional disparities for all sorts of  reasons it's why we've got the education  of the 55  educational investment areas up and down  the country in part it's because there  are some areas of the country where we  find it challenging to attract and  recruit teachers especially high caliber  and high quality teachers so for example  we have the grants in place in some of  those educational attainment areas in  some cases it's around early years the  importance of the the sort of one to one  to five but it but we continue to focus  on it because it's huge it's hugely  important that wherever you live is part  of our leveling up agenda wherever you  live in this country you should have  access to a world-class education a  fantastic school in college and go on to  achieve whatever you uh you know your  your uh uh your your potential okay now  i'm sure some of our viewers watching  this morning will like me have gone  through the three-day week and the power  blackouts in the 1970s the latest i'm  hearing is that some schools might only  be able to run three days a week because  they cannot afford the energy bills what  are you doing to help them  so a number of things so the first thing  is additional funding so an extra seven  billion pounds over this spending review  period an extra four billion pounds uh  this year so significant just for energy  bills no of course it's not so so it's  about giving them energy bills and and  also um  increase increase to uh staffing and  staff wages so we keep a watchful eye i  mean some schools will be uh under under  long-term contracts others will be  coming out of those contracts i'm  keeping a watchful eye i listen to  schools and school leaders up and down  the country and as i say we'll be  working with the new prime minister and  the new secretary of state and the new  government which we'll have in just a  couple of weeks time uh to look at some  of the challenges we face and what we  may need to do to acknowledge that some  schools might not be able to open five  days a week so no i don't accept that  and we we expect schools to be open five  days a week and over 70 percent of  schools already do a 32.5 hour week um  the ifs indeed have looked at schools  and and they they have said that um  schools schools should be able to manage  within their existing budgets but look  i'm alive to the fact that energy costs  are increasing uh at a significant rate  we see that in our household bills it  will be the same in uh commercial and  indeed schools so keep a very watchful  eye and continue to work  yeah i saw bruce johnson yesterday had  gone to ukraine to offer  his support the six-month  anniversary that's the right word of the  invasion  by russia and he says that we need to  continue on the path that we're on  because they're paying in blood we're  paying in higher bills what if people  can't afford to pay their higher bills  well i i know how concerned people are  especially ahead of the price cap  announcement  tomorrow uh what i would say to them is  there is already a 37 billion pound  package of support that's been put in  place not enough  well most of which hasn't actually come  into effect yet so the 400 pounds for  every household we've had the first 300  pound cost of living payment there will  be a second one towards the end of this  year 150 pounds for pensioners 150  pounds for those who on  disability benefits and that's exactly  why the government has always said and  indeed both of the leadership contenders  have said that their first act will be a  uh fiscal event where they will look at  what more support we can provide to  people but as i said that 37 billion  pound package support is in place 400  pounds for every household coming in the  autumn but i know how worried people are  about it and more support will come and  i say i'm pleased to see that both  leadership contenders and we'll have a  new prime minister in just a couple of  weeks time their number one priority  alongside their chancellor will be to  support people through this now i know  that you um you're not really got  involved in the leadership debate and  sorry you support rishi sooner but i've  not heard you speak too much about it  but i do want to put this to you because  you are a conservative senior minister  um a senior ally of liz trust has said  this morning that sunak is thrashing  around all over the place like a wounded  stoat his attacks um a weasel if you  don't know what one is his attacks are  framing us as taury scum  this is not what you need is it well i i  haven't i haven't seen seen this in the  times happening well i haven't seen i  haven't seen those comments as it was  early start this morning so i haven't  read okay i've read the times check my  word for what i will do but what i would  say on that is what one of the things  that i found most difficult about the  leadership contest has been the sort of  blue on blue which i think is totally  unnecessary both candidates should just  present their positive vision for our  country and the most important thing is  that in two weeks time so on on tuesday  the sixth when we have a new prime  minister that we all as a parliamentary  party get behind that new leader get  behind that prime minister and we  continue to deliver for the british  people that's what i want to see do you  think boris johnson has done enough in  this period between him stepping down  and the new minister the new prime  minister taking over it's been described  as a zombie government on more than one  occasion yeah i i don't recognize that  that picture and certainly from an  education perspective we've been working  hard on exam results the work that we'd  started around the send review around  the uh the green paper the work we're  doing around reforming children social  care that has all continued and it will  continue to continue and hard-working  civil servants alongside ministers up  and down  whitehall are working to ensure that the  new government will be it's not just  going to be starting from fresh they  will have a suite of options that  they'll be able to take forward  alongside ongoing 2019 manifesto  commitments that we are yet to deliver  and working hard on so i don't recognize  the picture that's painted and as far as  i'm concerned the prime minister has  always shown a massive interest in  education and what i've been doing both  in the children families brief and also  in the schools brief and i've no doubt  he'd continue to do so even though he's  working from home his country home  mostly  let's end where we started and uh a word  for those that don't get the gcse  results they're hoping for so don't  panic it really isn't the end of the  world there have never been more options  for post 16 than there are now so  whether you go on to do a levels t  levels which is our exciting new uh  technical vocational qualifier  qualification um whether you go on to  further education whether you go on uh  to an apprenticeship which is probably  more and more popular  there are so many options to you don't  panic speak to your college uh speak  speak to your parents uh you will be  able to achieve your potential just  don't panic how did you do  i it was a long time ago i got six a's  and four b's as i did as i recorded um  a levels didn't go quite quite so well  right but i really enjoyed my time at  school and that's the most important  thing you know your grades are important  but they don't define you uh just have a  plan b i hope you all young people down  in the country get the grades they want  to need today if you don't don't panic  there are so many options open to you  good to see you mister thanks very much

